imming should they be doing that  should they be staying here well i think  it's about common sense following the  the advice a public health advice from  the uk hsa  in terms of hydration a cover  covering up being in the shade avoiding  the times of the day when the heat is at  its peak there's a particular message  particularly for  for teenagers children some of those who  may be tempted to go for a swim  there's significant dangers of that  quite often when people go swimming in  rivers  when we have very hot weather so so it's  following common sense steps uh keeping  an eye on neighbours and those who are  vulnerable and following the guidance  that's been put out by the relevant  bodies what about schools are you  picking up any evidence of schools not  opening today what's the latest advice  from your from your point of view and  what's the latest situation in terms of  pupils being able to go to school well  it's important we get the balance right  with schools that uh some stay open for  key workers not least in the nhs so it's  important that schools do continue to be  open but at the same time head teachers  are empowered to make local decisions as  fits the needs of their local schools so  the department for education has put out  guidance to head teachers on the  practical steps that can be taken by  schools obviously it is a local decision  for them to take but at the same time we  need to get the balance right in terms  of making sure that for key workers  there is education continues to be on  offer in order that nhs staff in  particular can continue to get to work  kit multiples has also been sharing  these cobra meetings we've seen the pm  having a party yesterday at farnborough  air show today we've obviously got this  big internal party leadership contest  going on are you confident that the  government  is on top of this and is able to deliver  on these big priorities for the public  and deal with this heat wave and  seriousness of it given al